{{also|sublimé}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/səˈblaɪm/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-sublime.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪm}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|sublimen}}, borrowed from {{bor|en|fro|sublimer}}, from {{der|en|la|sublimō||to raise on high; to sublimate {{q|in Medieval Latin}}}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|sublim}}

# {{lb|en|chemistry|physics|ambitransitive}} To [[sublimate]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To raise on high.
#* {{rfdate|en|and other bibliographic particulars, eg, title of work, page, url, full name of author}} {{w|E. P. Whipple}}
#*: a soul '''sublimed''' by an idea above the region of vanity and conceit
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To exalt; to heighten; to improve; to purify.
#: {{syn|en|sublimate|q1=archaic}}
#* {{rfdate|en|and other bibliographic particulars, eg, title of work, page, url}} {{w|Alexander Pope}}
#*: The sun {{...}} / Which not alone the southern wit '''sublimes''', / But ripens spirits in cold, northern climes
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To dignify; to ennoble.
#* {{rfdate|en|and other bibliographic particulars, eg, title of work, page, url, full name of author}} {{w|Jeremy Taylor}}
#*: An ordinary gift cannot '''sublime''' a person to a supernatural employment.

