===Etymology 2===
From {{der|en|frm|sublime}}, from {{der|en|la|sublīmis||high}}, from {{m|la|sub-||up to, upwards}} + a root of uncertain affiliation often identified with {{der|en|la|līmis}}, ablative singular of {{m|la|līmus||oblique}} or {{m|la|līmen||threshold, entrance, lintel}}

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er}}

# [[noble|Noble]] and [[majestic]].
#* {{rfdate|en|and other bibliographic particulars, eg, title of work, page, url}} {{w|Thomas De Quincey}}
#*: the '''sublime''' Julian leader
# [[impressive|Impressive]] and [[awe-inspiring]], yet [[simple]].
#: {{ux|en|'''sublime''' scenery}}
#: {{ux|en|a '''sublime''' deed}}
#* {{rfdate|en|and other bibliographic particulars, eg, title of work, page, url}} {{w|Matthew Prior}}
#*: Easy in words thy style, in sense '''sublime'''.
#* {{rfdate|en|and other bibliographic particulars, eg, title of work, page, url}} {{w|Henry Wadsworth Longfellow}}
#*: Know how '''sublime''' a thing it is / To suffer and be strong.
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=1993| year_published=1995| author=Richard Klein| isbn=0330340166| publisher=Picador| location=London| title=Cigarettes are sublime| page=62| passage=Cigarettes are poison and they taste bad; they are not exactly beautiful, they are exactly '''sublime'''.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Lifted up; high in place; exalted aloft; uplifted; lofty.
#* {{rfdate|en|and other bibliographic particulars, eg, title of work, page, url, full name of author}} {{w|Dryden}}
#*: '''Sublime''' on these a tower of steel is reared.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Elevated by joy; elated.
#* {{rfdate|en|and other bibliographic particulars, eg, title of work, page, url}} {{w|John Milton}}
#*: Their hearts were jocund and '''sublime''', / Drunk with idolatry, drunk with wine.
# Lofty of mien; haughty; proud.
#* {{rfdate|en|and other bibliographic particulars, eg, title of work, page, url}} {{w|Edmund Spenser}}
#*: countenance '''sublime''' and insolent
#* {{rfdate|en|and other bibliographic particulars, eg, title of work, page, url}} {{w|John Milton}}
#*: His fair, large front and eye '''sublime''' declared / Absolute rule.

